      <div class="section-inner">
        <h2>Pros, Cons, and Who It Suits</h2>

        <div class="pros-cons-grid">
          <div class="pros-card">
            <h3 class="pros-heading">What it does well</h3>
            <ul class="verdict-list verdict-list--pros">
              {aorkuler.pros.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="cons-card">
            <h3 class="cons-heading">Trade-offs to know</h3>
            <ul class="verdict-list verdict-list--cons">
              {aorkuler.cons.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="best-for-list">
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--yes">
            <span class="bfi-icon">✓</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">Rural property and campsite owners</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">A few hundred feet of reliable finding, with no cell service and no monthly bill, is exactly the shape of this product.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--yes">
            <span class="bfi-icon">✓</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">People who refuse subscriptions on principle</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">Three years in, Aorkuler has cost you $249.99 and a cellular tracker has cost you roughly $400–450. If that maths is the deciding factor, this is the category winner.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--yes">
            <span class="bfi-icon">✓</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">Owners of a dog with a recall problem, not an escape problem</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">If your dog disappears into the treeline on walks and comes back eventually, this shortens the search. Natalie found Rhys quickly with it and would buy one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--no">
            <span class="bfi-icon bfi-icon--no">✗</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">Anyone who needs to be told their dog is gone</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">There are no geofences and no alerts. The controller sits in a drawer until you pick it up, by which point you already know. That is Fi's job.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--no">
            <span class="bfi-icon bfi-icon--no">✗</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">Hunters and working-dog handlers</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">Multi-dog tracking at genuine distance with maps on the handheld is Garmin territory, and our range result does not come close.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="best-for-item best-for-item--no">
            <span class="bfi-icon bfi-icon--no">✗</span>
            <div>
              <p class="bfi-label">Anyone who wants location history or sharing</p>
              <p class="bfi-desc">No app means no map, no breadcrumb trail, and no way for a second person to help search from their phone.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Comparisons -->
    <section class="content-section" id="comparisons">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <h2>Aorkuler vs Fi, Garmin, and AirTag</h2>
        <p class="section-body">
          These are not four versions of the same product, so there is no single winner.
          But there is a clear winner in each situation, and in one of them it is the
          Aorkuler outright.
        </p>

        <div class="verdict-grid">
          <div class="verdict-card verdict-card--win">
            <p class="vc-scenario">Subscription-free tracking under $300</p>
            <p class="vc-winner">Aorkuler wins</p>
            <p class="vc-body">
              Nothing else in this comparison does it. Fi structurally cannot — the
              subscription <em>is</em> the network. A Garmin Alpha system does it, but
              the collar and handheld together cost multiples of this. If "no monthly
              bill, works without cell service, under $300" is your brief, Aorkuler is
              the answer and it is not particularly close.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="verdict-card">
            <p class="vc-scenario">Suburban dog, escape alerts, phone in your pocket</p>
            <p class="vc-winner">Fi wins</p>
            <p class="vc-body">
              Fi pushes you a notification the moment your dog crosses a geofence, shows
              a live map, and needs nothing in your hand. Aorkuler offers none of that.
              If you have LTE where your dog actually goes, this is not a close call
              either. See our <a href={ROUTES.fiCollarReview} data-track="collector_to_converter_click" data-from-page={pageSlug} data-to-page={ROUTES.fiCollarReview} data-link-position="comparisons">Fi collar review</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="verdict-card">
            <p class="vc-scenario">Backcountry, multiple dogs, real distance</p>
            <p class="vc-winner">Garmin wins</p>
            <p class="vc-body">
              Miles of VHF range, up to 20 dogs, maps on the handheld. It costs several
              times as much and is a more complicated system to learn, but it is the
              proven option at distance — and distance is exactly what we did not verify
              on the Aorkuler. See <a href={ROUTES.garminTracking} data-track="collector_to_converter_click" data-from-page={pageSlug} data-to-page={ROUTES.garminTracking} data-link-position="comparisons">Garmin off-grid systems</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="verdict-card">
            <p class="vc-scenario">A cheap backup layer in a city</p>
            <p class="vc-winner">AirTag wins, on price</p>
            <p class="vc-body">
              Different category: a crowd-sourced item finder that only updates when
              someone else's iPhone walks past. Useless in a field, occasionally
              brilliant in a dense neighbourhood. See <a href={ROUTES.airtagForDogs} data-track="collector_to_converter_click" data-from-page={pageSlug} data-to-page={ROUTES.airtagForDogs} data-link-position="comparisons">AirTags for dogs</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

